.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt TBL 7
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm tbl
.Nd tables in manual pages
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
language describes a table inside a manual page written in
.Xr mdoc 7
or
.Xr man 7 .
It began as a preprocessor that turned tables into
.Xr roff 7
requests;
.Xr oxdoc 1
reads it directly, as part of the page.
.Pp
A table starts with a
.Ic \&TS
line and ends with a
.Ic \&TE
line.
Between them are, in order:
.Bl -enum -offset indent -compact
.It
an optional line of options, ending in a semicolon;
.It
the layout: one or more rows of column descriptions, the last ending in a
period;
.It
the data: one line per row, its cells separated by tabs.
.El
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.TS
box tab(:);
lb lb
l n.
Name:Size
kernel:31
modules:7
\&.TE
.Ed
.Pp
The layout's rows describe the data's rows in turn, and its last row
describes all the rest.
A line
.Ic \&T&
among the data starts a new layout for the rows after it, up to the next
period.
.Pp
In
.Xr man 7 ,
a table is set off from the text before it by a blank line; in
.Xr mdoc 7 ,
it is not.
A table's lines are not formatted as text: a macro line inside a table is
reported and ignored, as described under
.Sx DATA .
.Sh OPTIONS
Options are words, separated by spaces or commas, some with an argument in
parentheses.
Case does not matter.
.Bl -tag -width "decimalpoint(c)"
.It Cm allbox
Draw a box around the table and lines between all its cells.
.It Cm box , Cm frame
Draw a box around the table.
.It Cm center , Cm centre
Centre the table between the current indentation and the right margin.
A table too wide for that space moves left of the indentation.
.It Cm decimalpoint Ns Pq Ar c
Align numbers at
.Ar c
instead of a period.
.It Cm doublebox , Cm doubleframe
Draw a double box around the table.
.It Cm tab Ns Pq Ar c
Separate cells with
.Ar c
instead of a tab.
.El
.Pp
The options
.Cm delim ,
.Cm expand ,
.Cm linesize ,
.Cm nokeep ,
.Cm nospaces
and
.Cm nowarn
are accepted and have no effect.
.Sh LAYOUT
Each layout row is a list of column keys, each followed by its modifiers;
spaces between them are optional.
Rows are separated by commas or line ends, and the layout ends with a
period.
.Ss Keys
.Bl -tag -width 4n
.It Cm l
Left-aligned text.
.It Cm r
Right-aligned text.
.It Cm c
Centred text.
.It Cm n
A number: the numbers in the column are aligned at their decimal point, or
at
.Ql \e&
where a cell contains one, or else after their last digit.
A cell without a digit is centred.
If the column is wider than its numbers need, they are centred in it
together.
.It Cm a
Left-aligned text, indented by one column.
.It Cm s
The cell to the left spans this column too.
.It Cm ^
The cell above spans this row too; this row's own data for the column is
ignored.
.It Cm _ , Cm \-
A horizontal line across the cell.
.It Cm =
A double horizontal line across the cell.
.It Cm \&|
A vertical line between the columns on either side, or at the table's
edge; two for a double line.
.El
.Ss Modifiers
.Bl -tag -width 4n
.It Cm b
Bold.
.It Cm i
Italic.
.It Cm f Ns Ar name
The font
.Ar name ,
one or two characters:
.Cm B , I , BI , R ,
or a number from 1 to 4.
Fonts replace each other: the last one given counts.
.It Cm e
All columns marked
.Cm e
are as wide as the widest of them.
.It Cm x
The column takes a share of the width the other columns leave on the
line.
.It Cm w Ns Pq Ar width
The column is at least
.Ar width
wide, in the scaling units of
.Xr man 7 ,
columns by default.
A width that is not a number is ignored.
.It Ar number
The space after the column, in columns; three by default.
.El
.Pp
The modifiers
.Cm d , p , t , u , v
and
.Cm z
are accepted and have no effect on a terminal.
.Sh DATA
Each data line is a row, its cells separated by the tab character.
A cell's text is formatted as in a text line: escape sequences work, and
spaces at its edges are kept.
Cells beyond the layout row's columns are reported and ignored.
.Pp
A line consisting of
.Ql _
or
.Ql =
alone draws a single or double line across the table.
A cell consisting of one of them draws a line across the cell, reaching the
vertical lines beside it;
.Ql \e_
draws one as wide as the column, and
.Ql \e^
leaves the cell for the one above to span.
.Pp
A cell holding
.Ql T{
at the end of a line starts a text block: the lines after it, up to one
starting with
.Ql T} ,
are filled into a paragraph as wide as the column.
More cells may follow
.Ql T}
on its line.
A text block's column is at most a fraction of the line wide: the line
divided by one more than the number of columns.
.Pp
A macro line in a table is not formatted.
.Ic \&br ,
.Ic \&sp ,
.Ic \&ce
and
.Ic \&rj
leave nothing; any other macro leaves its arguments as text, as a row of
their own or, inside a text block, as part of the block.
Definitions of strings, registers and macros take effect, and macros the
page defines are expanded first.
.Sh EXAMPLES
A table of numbers under a spanning heading:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.TS
center tab(:);
cb s
l n.
Daily intake (MJ)
_
Carbohydrates:4.5
Fats:2.25
Protein:3
\&.TE
.Ed
.Pp
is shown as
.TS
center tab(:);
cb s
l n.
Daily intake (MJ)
_
Carbohydrates:4.5
Fats:2.25
Protein:3
.TE
.Pp
A boxed table with a text block:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.TS
allbox tab(:);
lb lb
l lw(30).
Option:Meaning
\-s:T{
Start in single-user mode, with a shell on the console.
T}
\-h:Use the serial console.
\&.TE
.Ed
.Pp
is shown as
.TS
allbox tab(:);
lb lb
l lw(30).
Option:Meaning
\-s:T{
Start in single-user mode, with a shell on the console.
T}
\-h:Use the serial console.
.TE
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
In lint mode,
.Xr oxdoc 1
reports problems in tables at the levels
.Xr mandoc 1
uses: unknown options and missing or wrong option arguments, invalid
characters and unmatched parentheses in the layout, an empty layout, a
layout row starting with a span, extra cells, data in a cell spanned from
above, a text block still open at
.Ic \&TE ,
a table without data, and ignored macros.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr man 1 ,
.Xr oxdoc 1 ,
.Xr eqn 7 ,
.Xr man 7 ,
.Xr mdoc 7 ,
.Xr roff 7
